Pelvic Floor Dyssynergia
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Excessive straining during defecation, Sensation of anorectal obstruction, Need for digital manual extraction, Paradoxical contraction on anorectal manometry

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-037
Reason for Visit: Severe, agonizing lower pelvic pain and absolute inability to defecate despite intense, prolonged straining, causing severe distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing severe acute pelvic pain and a sensation of total anorectal obstruction, leading to extreme distress and fear of a mechanical bowel impaction. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to physically perform a digital rectal examination, definitively rule out a hard faecal impaction or massive obstructing tumour, and safely manage the acute pain crisis prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've been on the toilet for three hours pushing so hard I feel dizzy, but the exit is completely blocked tight, and I'm in agony."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a severe exacerbation of chronic, refractory constipation characterized by incredibly difficult and prolonged defecation. Tonight, the patient has experienced excessive, painful, and intense straining. The patient describes a subjective sensation of severe incomplete rectal evacuation and a persistent, physical blockade right at the anal sphincter. The patient admits to the absolute necessity of utilizing digital manual perineal pressure to attempt to physically extract stool, but tonight this has failed. The patient notes a complete lack of response to high-dose osmotic laxatives taken over the last two days.
Functional Status: Exhausted, experiencing severe pelvic ache, pacing the bathroom.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme straining poses a severe risk of vasovagal syncope; patient is highly distressed and isolated.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Flushed, visibly uncomfortable, frequently shifting weight, holding the lower pelvis.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft, non-distended, with active bowel sounds. No palpable abdominal masses or peritonism. Digital rectal examination (DRE) reveals a tight, hypertonic external anal sphincter. The rectal vault contains soft, normal-consistency stool right at the anal verge, ruling out a hard, rock-like faecal impaction. However, during attempted simulated defecation, there is a paradoxical contraction and complete failure of relaxation of the puborectalis muscle against the examining finger. Absence of a true structural mechanical obstruction (no large prolapse or massive tumour). Documented history of paradoxical sphincter contraction on formal high-resolution anorectal manometry.
Environmental Observations: Bathroom contains multiple empty boxes of fibre supplements and enemas.
Medication Reconciliation: Overusing stimulant laxatives which are exacerbating colonic cramping against a closed sphincter.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pelvic Floor Dyssynergia - Status: Acute hypertonic spastic episode
Differentials:

Hard Faecal Impaction - Ruled out directly by DRE revealing soft, pliable stool at the anal verge, not a hard, unyielding mass.

Colorectal Carcinoma (Obstructing) - Ruled out by the historically normal colonoscopy, normal DRE lacking a fixed mass, and known manometry diagnosis.

Mechanical Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out by completely flat, soft abdomen, active bowel sounds, and lack of vomiting.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to severe, agonizing pelvic pain and functional incapacitation resulting from an absolute inability to defecate. The clinical presentation strongly mimicked an acute structural obstruction or severe faecal impaction. An immediate, hands-on clinical evaluation, strictly including a digital rectal examination, was mandatory to clinically rule out a rock-hard impaction or obstructing rectal mass. By confirming the presence of soft stool blocked solely by a paradoxically contracting, hypertonic sphincter, the physician safely diagnosed a functional dyssynergic flare, preventing a highly distressed and unnecessary presentation to the emergency department for surgical review. This fulfills the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a single Diazepam 5mg rectally (off-label use of oral tablet for local absorption) to force skeletal muscle relaxation of the external sphincter. Advised immediate cessation of harsh stimulant laxatives.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instead of holding your breath and straining forcefully (Valsalva), sit on the toilet with knees elevated above hips using a stool, and consciously blow air out slowly through pursed lips like inflating a balloon. This specific breathing technique prevents the diaphragm from bearing down aggressively, which reflexively forces the pelvic floor muscles to relax and open, rewarding the patient with successful, painless evacuation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized pelvic floor physiotherapist for intensive biofeedback retraining.
DME/Safety (Optional): Squatty Potty or toilet footstool.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to adjust osmotic laxatives and review biofeedback progress.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing a high fever, sudden rigid and severe abdominal pain, bilious (green) vomiting, or passage of large amounts of bright red blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on the previously confirmed manometry study; if severe, unremitting upper abdominal pain develops, re-evaluation is required.
Clinical Course: Dyssynergia means your pelvic muscles are confused; instead of relaxing to let stool out, they are squeezing shut when you push. Because the door is locked, pushing harder only causes more pain.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if you develop a high fever, start vomiting green fluid, or if your stomach becomes hard as a board and extremely painful to touch.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The stool in your rectum is already soft; you do not have a hard blockage. The intense pain is because your sphincter muscle is clamping shut like a fist while your bowel tries to push. Taking more laxatives just forces the bowel to cramp harder against a locked door.
Actionable Steps: Use a footstool to elevate your knees every time you sit on the toilet. This specific physical posture straightens the angle of your rectum and mechanically takes the tension off the puborectalis muscle, allowing gravity to assist rather than fighting your own anatomy.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Pelvic Floor Dyssynergia (muscle spasm). Exam shows soft stool, NO hard impaction. Stop straining. Use a footstool. See GP in 1 week.

SMS EDUCATION

Breathe out slowly like blowing a balloon when on the toilet; do NOT hold your breath and push. Call 000 if you get a fever or vomit green fluid.
